  "verbalization": "Pentoxyverine is implicated in the pathogenesis of rhinitis through a chain of indications that connect it to common cold symptoms and ultimately to rhinitis. The evidence suggests that Pentoxyverine may be associated with the common cold, which is a known trigger for rhinitis. This relationship is supported by multiple paths, each linking Pentoxyverine to a different symptom of the common cold, which in turn is linked to rhinitis. The integration of these pathways implies that Pentoxyverine may exert some effect on the immune response or inflammatory processes that underlie rhinitis. The scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, though the exact mechanism remains unclear. The presence of multiple indirect pathways supports the hypothesis that Pentoxyverine could play a role in the development of rhinitis, though further investigation is needed to elucidate the precise biological mechanisms involved.",
